+// The index of the tab `delta` steps away from `start`, skipping every tab
+// `selectable` marks false, wrapping at both ends. `selectable[i]` says whether
+// tab i can be landed on at all -- the caller folds together collapsed groups
+// and workspace visibility before calling.
+//
+// THE BOUND IS THE WHOLE POINT. The loop this replaces in
+// TabStripModel::SelectRelativeTab is an unbounded `while` that skips collapsed
+// groups, and it is safe only because Chromium guarantees the active tab is
+// never in a collapsed group, so the walk always has somewhere to stop. That
+// guarantee does not survive Workspaces: until the active tab follows a
+// workspace switch (E7b), switching leaves the active tab tagged for a
+// workspace that is not the active one, and then *every* entry here is false.
+// An unbounded walk in that state hangs the browser rather than crashing it,
+// which is far harder to diagnose than a crash and reaches the user as a
+// freeze on Ctrl+Tab. So this takes at most `selectable.size()` steps.
+//
+// Returns `start` unchanged when nothing is selectable, when `selectable` is
+// empty, when `start` is out of range, or when `delta` is zero. Staying put is
+// always safe, and there is no better answer when no tab may be shown --
+// Chromium's own comment on that loop calls falling back to the active tab the
+// intended behaviour.
+//
+// `start` itself is tested last, so a strip where only the active tab is
+// selectable returns `start` rather than wrapping past it.
+int NextVisibleTabIndex(const std::vector<bool>& selectable,
+                        int start,
+                        int delta);
